@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+decision: redesign
+triggers: shared-cause, defect-type
+summary: Six of ten r1 findings come from one decision: clone retirement and restoration are inferred each step from the pair (clone active?, seat Retired?) with no graph-side restore or purge intent. Replace that with a persisted lifecycle intent on CloneRecord that drives the unretire, succession, resume and finalize wants and gates the Retired→retire arm.
+pattern: The Blocking finding (undo re-retired by the next observation step) and the unretire-after-close race are mirror images. Both read the same state pair (clone active, seat Retired) as two opposite intents, because nothing records that undo happened. The missing succession want, the interim resume trigger that never holds (or that two launchers both fire on), and the SeatObservation wording on final retirement all come from the same gap: no record says "this clone is being restored onto seat S, pending X". Finalize-on-purge has the same flaw from the other side: an event-derived want with no surviving record to re-derive it after a failure. The remaining four findings (env delivery, pre-D1 precedence, unseated clone after restart, key-conflict alert lifecycle) are independent.
+clusters:
+- C1 lifecycle-intent: r1 [Blocking] design.md §6 Retired mapping; §8 "Undo commits the clone active"; Key abstractions SeatObservation + ThreadsSource unretire want, r1 [Should-fix] design.md Key abstractions ThreadsSource "unretire seat: for an active clone whose seat threads reports as reversibly retired"; §6 Retired mapping; §8 pane close, r1 [Should-fix] design.md §2 seat succession rule; §8 interim; Key abstractions ThreadsSource want list, r1 [Should-fix] design.md §9 resume driver trigger ("threads_seat is Unresolved and last bound pane id exists"); §8 Before D2 (interim), r1 [Should-fix] design.md §8 "When the graph's retirement becomes final (purge), the graph releases the key / finalizes retirement"; Key abstractions ThreadsSource "finalize retirement: on purge", r1 [Nit] design.md §3 "there is no separate 'retired-final' clone state"; §6 "Retired (reversible or final) → clone retired through the existing undoable retirement"; Key abstractions SeatObservation "final Retired retires it finally" | rule: every graph-initiated threads lifecycle command (unretire, succession, finalize) and the launch that follows a restore must be derived from a persisted graph-side intent on the clone that is cleared only on threads' acknowledgement, never from the bare pair (clone state, seat status). SeatObservation must not undo a pending intent.
+- C2 unseated or ambiguous threads answer before and after D1 (independent, local): r1 [Should-fix] design.md §6 "run alongside the old matcher (threads wins when both speak)"; Key abstractions SeatObservation "before D1, threads wins whenever it and the old matcher both claim a clone", r1 [Nit] design.md §2 resolve want precondition ("verified pane binding"); §6 "adopted only through the first bind of an unseated clone"; §9 driver trigger | rule: state for each SeatStatus, and for `threads_seat = None`, which source binds the clone and which rule launches it, both before and after D1 (Unresolved is not "threads speaking"; add a tie-break so one pane is never bound to two clones).
+changes: The implicit retire/restore protocol. It is stated in §6 ("Retired (reversible or final) → clone retired through the existing undoable retirement", unconditional), §8 and the Spec-forced exception ("Undo commits the clone active; unretire, relaunch and key steps follow from level-triggered wants. The undo code makes no threads calls"; "finalize retirement: on purge"), and the ThreadsSource want list (the unretire predicate "active clone whose seat threads reports as reversibly retired"; the resolve want only for "no threads_seat"). §9's trigger also does not cover restore.
+to: CloneRecord gains `seat_intent: Option<SeatIntent>`, written by the same commit that performs undo, resurrection or purge, and cleared by the threads executor once threads acknowledges. Variants: `Restore{seat, epoch}` and `Finalize{seat}`. Rules to write into the spec:
+(1) SeatObservation's Retired arm retires a clone only when it carries no `Restore` intent. A Retired seat on a clone with `Restore` is the expected interim state, not a retirement.
+(2) ThreadsSource derives from `Restore`: unretire, if the seat is reversibly retired (post-D2); or succession-resolve on the reopened pane written as the binding by the plan path, if the seat is final-retired (pre-D2, and operator-final after D2). ThreadsSeatAssignment admits succession only against a `Restore` intent. The intent clears when the clone's seat reads Unresolved or Resolved under the new or unretired seat.
+(3) The resume driver's trigger becomes "seat Unresolved, or the clone carries a `Restore` intent whose threads step is acknowledged", so on every restore path the driver is the single launcher, including after succession, when the new seat may already read Resolved with a bare shell.
+(4) There is no unretire want without a `Restore` intent, so an ordinary pane close is never reversed.
+(5) Purge writes `Finalize{seat}` and keeps the record as a tombstone. The finalize want is derived from it, and the record is removed only after threads acknowledges.
+§3's "no separate retired-final clone state" stands. The SeatObservation sentence "final Retired retires it finally" is replaced by rule (1).
+dissolves: [Blocking] design.md §6 Retired mapping; §8 "Undo commits the clone active"; Key abstractions SeatObservation + ThreadsSource unretire want, [Should-fix] design.md Key abstractions ThreadsSource "unretire seat: for an active clone whose seat threads reports as reversibly retired"; §6 Retired mapping; §8 pane close, [Should-fix] design.md §2 seat succession rule; §8 interim; Key abstractions ThreadsSource want list, [Should-fix] design.md §9 resume driver trigger ("threads_seat is Unresolved and last bound pane id exists"); §8 Before D2 (interim), [Should-fix] design.md §8 "When the graph's retirement becomes final (purge), the graph releases the key / finalizes retirement"; Key abstractions ThreadsSource "finalize retirement: on purge", [Nit] design.md §3 "there is no separate 'retired-final' clone state"; §6 "Retired (reversible or final) → clone retired through the existing undoable retirement"; Key abstractions SeatObservation "final Retired retires it finally"
+remains: [Should-fix] design.md §5 "argv prefix `env K=V …` or the existing pane-creation env, whichever the installed Herdr API supports"; Key abstractions StartAgent/HerdrApi adapter, [Should-fix] design.md §6 "run alongside the old matcher (threads wins when both speak)"; Key abstractions SeatObservation "before D1, threads wins whenever it and the old matcher both claim a clone", [Nit] design.md §2 resolve want precondition ("verified pane binding"); §6 "adopted only through the first bind of an unseated clone"; §9 driver trigger, [Nit] design.md §6 "key-conflict field → Unknown plus a recorded alert in `status`"; Key abstractions SeatObservation "The key-conflict alert is a journal entry"; Reconcile planner "skips clones that carry a key-conflict alert"
+scope: inside — goal clause "with graph undo restoring a closed clone onto the same seat", B7 ("retirement can happen immediately, but should be undoable") and B10 (threads restores the memberships on un-retire) stand unchanged. Only the graph-internal mechanism that carries them out changes.
+recommendation: Patching these one at a time means adding a precedence rule to SeatObservation, a separate epoch gate on the unretire want, a new succession want, a special case in the resume trigger and a purge tombstone. Each would rebuild part of the same missing intent record, and the next round would find the seams between them. One persisted intent field, owned by ThreadsSeatAssignment's neighbourhood and read by SeatObservation, ThreadsSource and the resume rule, resolves all six findings with a single rule, and it is about as small as the local fixes it replaces.
